Implement the following plan:

# M1: `aarch64-unknown-minixrs` target + ELF identity brand (+ close slice 5.3)

> **For agentic workers:** REQUIRED SUB-SKILL: Use superpowers:subagent-driven-development (recommended) or superpowers:executing-plans to implement this plan task-by-task. Steps use checkbox (`- [ ]`) syntax for tracking.

**Goal:** Move the 9 user-space binaries onto a custom `aarch64-unknown-minixrs` target (via `-Zbuild-std`), brand each with the normative minixrs ELF identity note, make the kernel refuse unbranded ELFs, and close slice 5.3's stale doc markers — all in one PR on branch `feature/m1-minixrs-target-brand`.

**Architecture:** The target JSON is the pinned nightly's `aarch64-unknown-none` spec plus `"os": "minixrs"` (disposable at M5). A zero-dep `minixrs-abi-note` crate exports a `brand!()` macro emitting the 28-byte PT_NOTE. The brand scanner is pure `no_std` code in `kernel-shared` (host-tested, used by the kernel at load time AND by `kernel/build.rs` at pack time). Kernel enforcement lands staged: warn-only commit, then hard-reject commit, same PR. The kernel itself stays on `aarch64-unknown-none`.

**Tech Stack:** Rust `nightly-2026-07-23` (pinned; `rust-src` + `llvm-tools` already in `rust-toolchain.toml`), cargo `-Zbuild-std`, lld linker scripts, QEMU aarch64.

**Normative references (read before deviating):**
- `~/src/tooling/docs/plans/minixrs-m1.md` — the M1 plan this executes
- `~/src/tooling/docs/abi-note.md` — note spec (byte layout, consumer/producer rules)
- `~/src/tooling/verify/check-brand.sh` — reference verifier (exit 0/1/2/3)

## Context

Slice 5.3 (TTY driver, PR #43) merged 2026-07-25 but its markers in `docs/plan.md:527` and `docs/plans/phase-5-musl-fs.md:607` still say `◀ ready (… pending merge)`. The user asked to close 5.3 and, instead of slice 5.4, execute the tooling repo's M1 plan — the first minixrs-repo milestone of the toolchain program (P0 done in `~/src/tooling`). M1's gate: QEMU boot green with all 9 binaries branded, kernel rejects unbranded, pack-time assertion active, CI green.

**User decisions (asked & answered):**
- 5.3 marker flip is bundled as the first commit of the M1 PR.
- Reject-path coverage = kernel-shared host unit tests + one documented manual QEMU mutation test. No permanent CI reject-boot scaffolding.

## Global Constraints

- Kernel stays on `aarch64-unknown-none`; `kernel/Cargo.toml` `forced-target`, `.cargo/config.toml` runner/alias, `clippy-kernel` + `qemu-smoke` CI jobs all unchanged.
- Target JSON: `llvm-target` stays `"aarch64-unknown-none"` (fork rustc is M5); the JSON + check-cfg shim must stay easy to delete at M5.
- Note bytes exactly per abi-note.md: owner `"minixrs\0"`, `namesz=8`, `descsz=8`, type `NT_MINIXRS_IDENT=1`, desc `[abi_version=1, flags=0]`, section `.note.minixrs.ident`, dedicated PT_NOTE + start of RO PT_LOAD.
- Consumer rules: walk PT_NOTE phdrs only; skip foreign owners; accept `descsz >= 8`; reject unknown `abi_version`.
- Every new `.rs` file starts with `// SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause` + `// Copyright (c) 2025-2026 Kevin Barnard and minix.rs Contributors`. `.ld`/`.json` files: no header.
- `kernel-shared` stays zero-`unsafe`; offset arithmetic uses `checked_*` (release has `overflow-checks = false`).
- Toolchain pin unchanged. No new external deps (deny/audit untouched).
- Staged kernel enforcement: warn commit and reject commit are separate, in the same PR; never land warn-only alone.
- Signed commits (never `--no-gpg-sign` / `--no-verify`). If running in auto-accept mode: STOP and surface staged changes before each `git commit`.
- Blocking gates before push: `cargo fmt --all --check`; `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings`; `cargo clippy -p minixrs-kernel --target aarch64-unknown-none -- -D warnings` (both default and `--no-default-features`); QEMU boot + `tools/check-boot-log.sh`.

**The 9 binary crates** (used throughout; "×9" means all of these): `servers/pm`, `servers/vfs`, `servers/ds`, `servers/sched`, `servers/rs`, `servers/vm`, `drivers/tty`, `userland/init`, `userland/worker`.

